    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I like the idea of a boolean flag for certainty. My other concern is the 'unidentified' part itself. Sometimes the records suggest these items were produced by groups or collectives that don't have a formal name, or they just seem to pop up in the archives with these strange, unexplained origins. In those cases where we're dealing with these almost 'elusive' or 'shadowy' sources that we can't pin down to a specific person or institution, are there any established taxonomies for classifying those kinds of highly obscure or mysterious entities?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For those elusive, shadowy sources, you are venturing into the territory of metadata as much as pure cataloging, and it requires a bit of creative taxonomy. Since standard controlled vocabularies often fail when the source is deliberately vague, I suggest creating a custom controlled vocabulary specifically for these \"collective/obscure\" entities. You could define terms like \"Anonymous Collective,\" \"Workshop Production,\" or \"Unattributed Source Group.\" This allows you to tag them consistently, which is far better than just writing \"some people\" in every record. It gives structure to the chaos, which is what good metadata should do.",
      "source": "target",
